  "prediction": "**Summary of USAID Assistance to the Palestinians in the West Bank and Gaza**\n\nThe United States Agency for International Development (USAID) has provided over $5 billion in bilateral assistance to the Palestinians in the West Bank and Gaza since 1993. The majority of this assistance was allocated through the Economic Support Fund (ESF) and was used to support various development objectives, including economic growth, infrastructure development, and governance.\n\nAs of March 31, 2018, USAID had obligated approximately $544.1 million (over 99 percent) and expended about $350.6 million (over 64 percent) of the ESF assistance allocated for the West Bank and Gaza in fiscal years 2015 and 2016. The assistance was used to support projects in areas such as health, water and road infrastructure, local governance, civil society, rule of law, education, and youth development.\n\nUSAID also made direct payments to Palestinian Authority (PA) creditors totaling about $75 million from fiscal year 2015 funds and $70 million from fiscal year 2016 funds. The agency vetted each PA creditor to ensure that the assistance would not provide support to entities or individuals associated with terrorism.\n\nThe report found that USAID complied with the requirements for vetting PA creditors and assessing the PA's financial systems. However, external assessments identified weaknesses in the PA's medical referral services and petroleum procurement system. USAID took steps to mitigate these findings, including commissioning financial audits and implementing additional controls.\n\nThe report concludes that USAID's assistance to the Palestinians in the West Bank and Gaza has contributed to building a more democratic, stable, prosperous, and secure Palestinian society. However, the agency must continue to ensure that its assistance is used effectively and efficiently, and that it complies with all relevant laws and regulations.\n\n**Key Findings:**\n\n* USAID obligated and expended over $544 million in ESF assistance for the West Bank and Gaza in fiscal years 2015 and 2016.\n* USAID made direct payments to PA creditors totaling about $145 million.\n* USAID vetted each PA creditor to ensure that the assistance would not provide support to entities or individuals associated with terrorism.\n* External assessments identified weaknesses in the PA's medical referral services and petroleum procurement system.\n* USAID took steps to mitigate these findings, including commissioning financial audits and implementing additional controls.\n\n**Recommendations:**\n\n* USAID should continue to ensure that its assistance is used effectively and efficiently.\n* USAID should continue to comply with all relevant laws",
